/* Generated from the 6 Nov 2026 Guthrie review consilium. Do not edit by hand. */
window.GUTHRIE_REVISIONS = {"lesson-history-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"People were drawn to IFS because it did not pathologise mental problems, because it made Self central, and because it helped explain all human systems, internal and external. This course uses those educational and experiential layers. It is, however, neither psychotherapy nor practitioner training.","comment":""},"lesson-history-block-14":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"A map of parts and Self interacting within a system.\n\nA way to say “a part of me” and to meet inner distress with compassion rather than avoidance.","comment":""},"lesson-history-block-16":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"IFS shifts from asking, what's wrong with me? to asking, what does that part of me need?","comment":""},"lesson-history-block-07":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"Delete"},"lesson-history-block-06":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"Delete this section. It has been integrated above."},"lesson-history-block-08":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"Delete"},"lesson-history-block-09":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"Delete"},"lesson-history-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"And every person has Self — a core capacity to relate to distress, internal and external, with calm, clarity and care.","comment":""},"lesson-history-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Internal Family Systems (IFS) is best known as a therapeutic model, but it is much more than that -- it is a programme for healing and awakening. In this course, we use its basic ideas to understand the human mind and why we do the things we do, even when they are bad for us.\n\nRichard C. Schwartz began developing IFS in the early 1980s, when he noticed that his clients referred to distinct inner voices. Instead of treating those voices as a problem, he listened to them and discovered that they resembled real people and, like people, they reacted to other voices in the client's mind. One part might want to do something, and another part would resist.\n\nAs a family therapist, Schwartz had been trained to look at families as systems: Who reacts to whom? What roles develop? How are patterns locked in? He then began to apply the same principles used with external families to the internal family of parts in his clients.\n\nSchwartz brought together two ideas with long separate histories: the mind is naturally multiple, and human behaviour makes more sense in relationship. Schwartz also gave that synthesis a distinctive centre. Alongside the parts, he discovered that every person has Self, which is whole, wise, loving and good.","comment":""},"lesson-history-block-10":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"What does the title Internal Family Systems mean?","comment":""},"lesson-history-block-11":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Dick Schwartz wondered what to call the model of the human mind that he was developing. His first insight was that the human mind is not unitary but multiple, that we have many sub-minds, or parts, that act just like people. They have ages, temperaments, talents, and worldviews. His second insight was that they behave toward each other like members of a family: agreeing, disagreeing, forming coalitions and getting into fights. In other words, the parts formed an internal family system.","comment":""},"lesson-history-block-12":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"IFS is different from ordinary family therapy, even though it grew out of that tradition. The “internal family” is the system of parts within a person.","comment":""},"lesson-history-block-15":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"It explains the model and develops a language for relating skilfully to yourself and others.","comment":""},"lesson-w1-after-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"After the live session, practise pausing briefly during the day to notice your state and shift into Presence. Repeat what helps; there is no list to complete.","comment":""},"lesson-w1-after-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The aim is not one dramatic experience. A brief glimpse, repeated many times, gradually helps you spend more time in a state of fundamental wellbeing.","comment":""},"lesson-w1-after-block-05":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"A state becomes useful when you can recognise it in ordinary life. Short repetitions usually teach more than one ambitious session. Practise at relatively calm moments rather than in the hardest hour of your week: before opening email, on a walk, before a meeting, or after closing your laptop.","comment":""},"lesson-w1-after-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Feel ground through feet or seat. Notice one sensation in your torso. Notice one sound. Open peripheral vision. Then focus on the next task. Twenty seconds is enough.","comment":""},"lesson-w1-after-block-09":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Use the seven-minute sequence two or three times during the week: ground, core, field, focus. Afterwards, write down only what you observed: “my jaw softened”, “thoughts kept moving”, “I heard more of the room”, “opening the field felt uncomfortable”. Accurate observation supports change; evaluation stops change.","comment":""},"lesson-w1-before-block-09":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The four building blocks of Presence","comment":""},"lesson-w1-before-block-13":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Calm is one possible quality of access to Self, not proof that you did the practice correctly. You may find curiosity while anxiety continues, or a little more room while a protector is still triggered. Guthrie describes access to Self as a continuum: ask how much access is available now, rather than whether you are finally “in Self”.","comment":""},"lesson-w1-before-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Arrive: Sitting comfortably upright with eyes closed. Relaxing body and mind.\n\nGround: Feeling your weight landing on your seat. If your feet are on the floor, feeling them flat on the floor. Letting gravity hold you. Surrendering to gravity.\n\nCore: Bringing your attention to your torso, to your core. Feeling into your body between seat and head. Being with the sensations in your core.\n\nField: Letting your awareness open into the room where you are. Noticing that awareness easily encompasses any sounds, near and far. Noticing that awareness can keep expanding, encompassing the solar system and beyond. Resting in the field of awareness: calm, spacious, silent, awake.\n\nField, part 2: Noticing that awareness encompasses the body and permeates the body.\n\nPrepare: Noticing that you have ground (a connection to the earth), core (a felt sense of your body) and field (panoramic awareness).\n\nFocus: From here you are ready to focus. You can now lightly place your attention on what is next.","comment":"Completely rewritten."},"lesson-w1-before-block-10":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Ground: Opening an energetic connection with the earth, with ground. Principal grounding points are feet and seat. When grounded, you have stability, stillness and a sense of safety.\n\nCore: Feeling into your trunk or core. The core of core is the column that runs from the crown of your head to the perineum (between anus and genitals) and includes the spine. When you have core, you know what you feel, want, or need. You have confidence; you have inner authority.\n\nField: Everything we notice shows up in the field of awareness: information from the five physical senses, emotions and thoughts. All those are objects of awareness that arrive and depart, while awareness itself remains. Awareness is like the sky, and sensations, emotions, and thoughts are like the weather. Awareness is always present; the contents of awareness are always changing.\n\nFocus: Skillful attention selects one object, task or person to highlight while ground, core and field remain in place.","comment":""},"lesson-support-block-05":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"A model of the human mind, a method of working with parts of the mind, a way of life, and, ultimately, a programme for changing the world.\n\nNarrow and selective focus on one or two things at a time.\n\nThe wider, inclusive field of consciousness in which all experience arises.\n\nAwake awareness, the field of consciousness, Presence. Characterised by the eight C's: calm, clarity, confidence, curiosity, compassion, creativity, connectedness, and courage.\n\nA sub-mind or sub-personality, with its own age, talents, temperament, and belief system.","comment":""},"lesson-support-block-08":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"People vary in how they respond to looking inside, feeling body sensations, meditating and using parts language.","comment":""},"lesson-support-block-10":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"You cannot reliably sense the room you are in or feel the support beneath you.","comment":""},"lesson-support-block-13":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"I would either delete this section or limit it to saying: Do not comment on other people's parts. Speak only of your own."},"lesson-guthrie-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"He co-created and co-led Coaching for Self-Leadership through the IFS Institute for more than a thousand participants and has studied extensively with the founding developer of IFS, Richard Schwartz.","comment":""},"lesson-guthrie-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Guthrie Sayen teaches IFS through the quality of contact he brings to the room. Concepts matter, but the first lesson is relational: what changes when a person is met with Presence?","comment":""},"lesson-guthrie-block-08":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Guthrie holds a PhD and is a Professional Certified Coach, a Level 3-trained IFS practitioner, and the founder of The Institute for Healing and Awakening.","comment":""},"lesson-guthrie-block-10":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"In this course, the two directions support each other: field awareness can make parts easier to meet, and a good relationship with parts can leave awareness less obscured.","comment":""},"lesson-guthrie-block-12":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Operating systems 1.0 and 2.0","comment":""},"lesson-guthrie-block-15":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The metaphor of two different OSs has a basis in neuroscience. Attention (focus) and awareness (field) operate off different sets of brain structures. Attention lives more in the left hemisphere, while drawing on the right hemisphere; awareness is just the reverse, living more in the right hemisphere with connections to the left.","comment":""},"lesson-guthrie-block-18":{"type":"approved","suggestion":"","comment":"Quotation is good as is and may used in anyway the team sees fit."},"lesson-ifs-block-06":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"IFS holds that everyone already has a core capacity that is whole and able to relate without force. Following Guthrie, this course also understands Self as awake awareness: the living capacity that can notice, include and relate holistically to what is happening internally, in relationship and in the environment.","comment":""},"lesson-ifs-block-10":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"1. We have many parts. Thoughts, feelings, impulses, images and body states indicate the presence of parts.\n\n2. All parts are good. A part's strategy may be harmful, but once we understand the part's secret history, we see its intention is positive, even noble.\n\n6. Trust the system and its pace. Pressure usually triggers more problematic behaviour. Patience, curiosity, and compassion are ultimately more efficient.","comment":""},"lesson-ifs-block-11":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The classic IFS model works from the same basic foundation: natural multiplicity, positive intention, systemic relationship, an empowering understanding of Self, and the possibility that parts can return to less extreme roles.","comment":""},"lesson-ifs-block-13":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The second statement does not eliminate responsibility for replying, but it does create more space inside you, and in that space lives compassion, and compassion helps us to respond creatively.","comment":""},"lesson-ifs-block-16":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"This distinction supports accountability. If a defensive part spoke harshly, “a part did it” is not an excuse. Rather it is a more precise starting point for repair: name the reaction, recognise what it was trying to protect, take responsibility for its impact, and choose what happens next.","comment":""},"lesson-w1-live-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Arrive: Guthrie takes us through the four building blocks of Presence: ground, core, field, focus. Each time the meditation will vary a little.\n\nExperience: \"Bowl of soup” exercise, using attention and awareness, followed by a full group debrief. It is introduced here, before it appears in the follow-up material.","comment":""},"lesson-w1-live-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Guthrie improvises within a structure, dancing with what is in the space. Participants are learning the four building blocks of Presence, the difference between attention (focus) and awareness (panorama), and a sense of parts. Participants are learning a way of being more than what to do.","comment":""},"lesson-w1-live-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The live call is not a lecture to transcribe. It is where the written material becomes experiential and where you begin to get a felt sense of a new way of being. Listen for Guthrie’s pacing, notice how he meets uncertainty, and watch how he returns to ground, core and field while staying in relationship.","comment":""},"lesson-w1-live-block-10":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Speak only from your own experience. It is enough to say what you felt and what you observed in Guthrie, the person with whom he was working, and changes in the group field. Do not interpret or diagnose anyone’s parts, and do not tell another participant what their experience meant.","comment":""},"lesson-w1-live-block-11":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"What did Guthrie do that made the room feel more aligned, spacious or safe?","comment":""},"lesson-start-block-07":{"type":"approved","suggestion":"","comment":"Quotation is good as is. Can be used anywhere."},"lesson-start-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"You know the difference between attention (selective) and awareness (inclusive) in your own experience and can recognise at least one sign that you have some access to Self.","comment":""},"lesson-start-block-16":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"What would make these six weeks worth your time?","comment":""},"lesson-start-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Week 1 begins with one practical distinction -- between attention (narrow) and awareness (broad). You will test that difference in your own experience and begin to recognise what access to Self may feel like for you.","comment":""},"lesson-start-block-03":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Brief shifts into wide awareness during the day, and one parts-language sentence used in an ordinary moment.","comment":""},"lesson-start-block-05":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"This course practises a second capacity: the wider knowing in which your experience, the presence of other people, and the environment can all be included simultaneously in your awareness. You move from narrow focus to panoramic awareness.","comment":""},"lesson-start-block-06":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Guthrie describes this as shifting from a thought-based operating system to an awareness-based one -- from OS 1.0 to OS 2.0. This shift is not achieved by willpower but by softening into a larger field of awareness. This does not mean throwing out attention or focus. The principle is transcend-and-include. Focus stays available and becomes a tool held inside a wider field, as we relax our exclusive reliance on thought.","comment":""},"lesson-start-block-09":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"This is an introduction to a new way of being and knowing. You will learn a simple framework for understanding the human mind, test that understanding in direct experience, and practise shifting into greater calm, clarity, and awareness. You will likely feel more freedom and joy. And this shift requires no effort, just softening into an intention.","comment":""},"lesson-start-block-12":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Week 1 begins with distinguishing between two ways of processing information: attention (narrow) and awareness (wide) and introduces Guthrie's four building blocks of Presence.\n\nWeek 2 introduces the idea that the human mind comprises many sub-minds, or parts, that are often in conflict but that we can befriend, which leads to greater harmony and joy.\n\nWeek 3 looks at Self and how to shift into calm, clarity, confidence and creativity.\n\nWeek 4 meets managers and firefighters, two classes of protectors who use opposing strategies to keep us safe.\n\nWeek 5 shows us how to be with vulnerable parts in a way that is safe and easy.\n\n Week 6 applies the learning to your life: making decisions, setting boundaries, managing conflict and repairing ruptures.","comment":""},"lesson-start-block-15":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"You may take the language of this course psychologically, spiritually, metaphorically or pragmatically; the course does not require a belief system. You may keep your eyes open, skip an exercise, or stop without explaining yourself; you are always at choice. You may also remain unconvinced. Curiosity is enough; agreement is not required.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-after-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Write down what the part seems to want you to know without correcting it.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-after-block-03":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"One situation, several parts","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-after-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Choose one situation where at least two parts have different views about what to do. Ask each part to complete: “I want…” and “I am afraid…” Do not choose a winner. The point is to let the system become more visible.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-after-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Possible intention: Ask the part, “How are you trying to help?” Then pause and listen for the answer to come from the concerned part rather than thinking of the answer.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-after-block-08":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Do not let this practice become onerous. Two or three entries in a week are enough. If the practice produces more self-criticism than information, name the part that is monitoring and make the task smaller.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-before-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"IFS sees the mind as naturally multiple. That multiplicity is normal, healthy, and beneficial.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-before-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Meet a part as someone with a point of view, as a person to be treated with respect, not as an object to be changed or eliminated.\n\nThere are no bad parts, only bad roles: a strategy may be costly, while the intention underneath it is positive, even noble.\n\nEnough space to notice a part and relate to it with respect.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-before-block-08":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"A part does not have to arrive as a clear voice or image. It may show up as a sensation in the body, an emotion, a thought, a reaction, or shift in perception. Some people visualise vividly; others know a part through thought or sensation alone. No channel is more authentic than another.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-before-block-11":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"Delete this paragraph."},"week-2-lesson-w2-before-block-14":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Treat all parts with unconditional positive regard. When they are approached with curiosity and compassion, they feel seen, understood, and appreciated, and they begin to change.","comment":"Replace this paragraph with the new text."},"week-2-lesson-w2-before-block-15":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Take one everyday reaction and list only what you can directly observe: words, emotion, body sensation, impulse, image, or where your attention went. Then try the sentence “a part of me…” and notice whether it creates even a little more space.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-before-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"A protective part may have its own age, personality, dreams, fears, and strategy. A critic may be trying to improve performance. A perfectionist may be trying to avoid shame. A pleaser may be taking care of a relationship. An angry part may be guarding a boundary, and an avoidant part may be preventing overwhelm. Understanding an intention is not the same as approving of the strategy and its effects. Understanding brings curiosity and compassion, which bring healing.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-before-block-10":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Parts generally show up as somatic sensations, emotions, thoughts, and impulses. Rather than making assumptions about which part may be present, simply notice what is here without judgement. Then ask what the sensation, emotion, or thought wants you to know.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-before-block-13":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"One fundamental principle of IFS is: All parts are good; there are not bad parts, only parts in bad roles. Some parts have been hurt, and they carry burdens of pain, shame, and fear. Those parts are not their burdens. Other parts have taken on extreme strategies to protect the hurt parts, but they are not those strategies. When parts are healed, they resume their natural, valuable roles as assets to the internal family.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-boundary-block-08":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Listening is not guiding. A course partner can reflect back what they heard, ask what support is wanted, and respect a stop. They should not lead imagery, search for origins, or negotiate with another participant’s protectors.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-boundary-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The phrase “a part of me” is intended to help us take more responsibility for our actions, not to become an excuse. You remain responsible for what you say and do, even when a protector is strongly blended.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-glossary-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Multiplicity describes the human mind as having many parts. Blending means that a part’s feelings or beliefs merge with your sense of who you are. Unblending creates enough separation for Self and the part to be in relationship. Polarisation is a conflict in which opposing parts become more extreme because each fears what the other would do if it relaxed.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-glossary-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The human mind has many parts or sub-minds, and we can talk to those parts just as we would a person.\n\nA conflict between parts with differing strategies to get what is best.\n\nAn extreme belief, emotion or role that a part carries; this burden governs its behaviour but is not its true nature.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-glossary-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Some of these books also describe advanced processes, such as retrieval and unburdening. Those belong to work with a qualified IFS practitioner and are not taught in this course.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-live-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The demonstration is not a protocol for participants to reproduce on one another. It's an example of how to be with a person and their parts.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-live-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Learn the quality of curiosity and the pacing. Participants are not being trained to guide one another through the IFS protocol.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-live-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Unblending is not pushing a part away. It is a respectful process to help a person separate from a part so that they can be in relationship with it. The reaction may still be present, but now there is more space. A change in wording can bring unblending, switching from “I am a failure” to “a part of me believes I failed”. A deepening and slowing of the breath can support unblending.  Curiosity -- I wonder what's at stake here? -- also supports unblending.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-live-block-06":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Is there enough access to Self to listen without one part taking control?","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-system-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"An inner conflict is a disagreement between parts about what is best for the internal system.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-system-block-06":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Those gifts become easier to access as burdens are released.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-system-block-11":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Change begins with understanding the dynamic rather than eliminating the behaviour that is most visible.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-system-block-13":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"In IFS, a burden is an extreme belief or emotion that attaches to a part after trauma or hurt. A burden is not the part’s essence. A part may believe it must never make a mistake or that it is unlovable, but the model holds that the part is more than the extreme role or the feeling. It is naturally good, valuable, and essential.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-system-block-14":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"In this introductory course we learn the concept and stop there. Unburdening requires more training is not taught here. What we are building is recognition and relationship.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-system-block-16":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Inner work should not convert every problem into a problem of individual psychology. Workload, unfair treatment, finances, unsafe relationships and cultural expectation can all constrain an inner system. A protector may be responding skilfully to a present reality rather than to an outdated fear. Self-leadership includes looking honestly at the environment and changing it where that is possible.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-system-block-17":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"If the part you like least disappeared tomorrow, which other part or which outside risk might become more prominent? The answer often shows what the visible strategy has been attempting to manage.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-start-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Before the live session, meet parts as people with their own points of view, learn what unblending means, and create a small map of the parts concerned with one issue rather than trying to inventory your whole inner system. During the live session, practise moving from being blended with a part to noticing it. Afterwards, use parts language in one ordinary situation.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-start-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"A cautious part remembers how tired you got the last time you took on a new role.","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-start-block-09":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Curiosity, a quality of Self, asks other questions:","comment":""},"week-2-lesson-w2-start-block-10":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The test is whether the language leaves you with more calm, clarity, confidence, and choice than you had before.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-after-block-06":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"This is not a search for origins and not a negotiation for change. It is a short experience of relationship. If the part becomes younger, more distressed or overwhelming, stop the inquiry and orient to the room you are in. Name three objects.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-after-block-03":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Write one thing your fixing part says, then one question that genuine curiosity might ask. Do not force the question if curiosity is not available. Simply noticing is the practice.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-after-block-05":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"5. Offer one sincere quality: curiosity, compassion, or availability.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-boundary-block-03":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Remember   The relationship changes before the part does. Curiosity without an agenda is already meaningful.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-boundary-block-06":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Use first-person, revisable language: “I notice more clarity”, “I may have some access to curiosity”, “a part of me is very certain”. In a conflict, consent to dialog and taking responsibility for what you say and do support safety.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-boundary-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Stay within capacity. Do not choose the most painful material available in order to prove that the practice works. If a part does not want attention, respect that response and return to the room.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-boundary-block-05":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"“I am speaking from Self” can become a status claim that places one person above another. It is also a sure sign that you are blended with a part. \n\nAccess to Self does not make a statement automatically correct, and being activated does not make a concern invalid.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-boundary-block-09":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Remember   Self is not a performance of serenity. Look for enough spaciousness, curiosity and connection to support the next responsible choice.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-before-block-01":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"Delete first paragraph"},"week-3-lesson-w3-before-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Calm   Internal peace, relaxed, serene, composed, equanimous.\n\nCuriosity   Genuine interest, seeking to understand, without agenda or judgement.\n\nClarity   Seeing things as they are, without distortion or judgement.\n\nCompassion   Acknowledging and being with suffering with no need to change anything while still being available to help if requested.\n\nConfidence   Trusting your capacity even in unfamiliar circumstances.\n\nCourage   Staying present and acting even while feeling fear.\n\nCreativity   Access to resources within and from the field of awareness.\n\nConnectedness  Unfettered openness with parts, with people and with the wider field.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-before-block-06":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The eight C's are most useful when seen as relational capacities, not as adjectives for a perfected person.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-before-block-09":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"If you ask yourself “Am I calm enough? Curious enough? Compassionate enough?”, a monitoring part may answer. Guthrie’s teaching includes quieter markers because access to Self is often modest: the breath is soft and slow, the voice is relaxed, the outcome seems less  urgent, you are willing to say “I don’t know.”","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-before-block-10":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Hold the C-qualities lightly, as references, not as a checklist. Ask after an event: Which one was available? Which was missing? What happened in the relational field? The aim is recognition, not a score.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-before-block-11":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Choose one part that is present but not overwhelming you, and one quality — curiosity, say, or clarity. Ask what 5 per cent more of that quality would look like as a behaviour. It might mean waiting ten seconds, asking a question, or admitting that you do not know.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-markers-block-02":{"type":"approved","suggestion":"","comment":"ok as is"},"week-3-lesson-w3-markers-block-05":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Opening 5 percent more space or asking a question from curiosity can be enough to access Self and for a different relationship to begin.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-markers-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Guthrie describes patterns across body, voice, attention and attitude. No single marker proves access to Self, and every marker is shaped by temperament, culture, ability, neurotype and current health. This is not a checklist to score yourself against; these are simply clues to what access to Self feels like.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-markers-block-11":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The point is not to become suspicious of competence or of calm. Ask relational questions instead: Can I receive a No? Can I admit to not knowing? Can I stay connected when the outcome changes? Can I be with the sensations in my body? Can I welcome parts when they show up?","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-markers-general":{"type":"approved","suggestion":"","comment":"the material in this module is can stay here. 12 oct 2026"},"week-3-lesson-w3-markers-block-01":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"The production note has been incorporated into the lesson title and introduction."},"week-3-lesson-w3-markers-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"A Self-like part can sound calm, compassionate and confident while still trying to control things. This is a protector working hard at its job -- to avoid pain. If you notice pressure to forgive, to be kind or to make another part disappear, name that pressure as a part. Nothing has gone wrong; you have found another part that needs unconditional positive regard.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-markers-block-08":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Body   Relaxed muscles in torso and face, easy breathing, feeling at home in the body.\n\nVoice   Relaxed throat, voice slightly lower, pacing is smooth and calm.\n\nAttention   Can hold focus comfortably while still having access to information coming from the body, a part, another person, and the environment.\n\nAttitude   A sense of being OK, less attachment to outcome, less judgement of self and others, more willingness to receive feedback.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-markers-block-13":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"When a part blends with Self, it has often concluded that leadership is unavailable. Criticising the part confirms its fear. The first movement is noticing: “This feels urgent; maybe, I am blended with a part.” Even that sentence may restore access to a modicum of Self.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-markers-block-14":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"You have access to Self when you can hold a perspective that differs from yours without getting defensive or aggressive. You can be curious, while still not agreeing. You are seeking understanding not a victory.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-live-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Loving presence shows up in behaviour. It looks like respecting a No, not arguing with a protector, distinguishing between a part's impact (negative) and its intention (positive), and letting go of attachment to a dramatic result.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-live-block-01":{"type":"approved","suggestion":"","comment":"The proposed title has been incorporated into the lesson heading."},"week-3-lesson-w3-live-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"A question can sound compassionate and still carry pressure. “What do you need?” sometimes means “I can solve your problem.” During the live work, pay attention to pace, tone, willingness to pause, and whether the person is genuinely free not to answer.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-live-block-08":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"What was the impact of the practitioner's presence? Where did more choice become available? What had the biggest impact? You are welcome to answer these in your own notes rather than aloud.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-start-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"You can recognise several markers indicating access to Self, and you can relate with curiosity and compassion to a part that is present.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-start-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Before live   Ask yourself, what is aware of my body sensations, emotions, and thoughts? Read the eight C's and notice body signals that you might have some access to Self.\n\nLive session   Feel the difference between attempting to ignore or manage a part and a relaxed acceptance of the part.\n\nAfter live   Offer a few minutes of curiosity or understanding to one part that is present without requiring it to change.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-start-block-03":{"type":"approved","suggestion":"","comment":"Quotation is good as is. Use wherever you think it works best."},"week-3-lesson-w3-start-block-04":{"type":"approved","suggestion":"","comment":"no changes"},"week-3-lesson-w3-start-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"A part can be identified quite accurately and still be met with judgement, impatience or analysis. “I know this is my critic” can mean “I have finally found the enemy.” IFS therefore asks a second question: “How do I feel toward this part?” The answer shows what relationship is available right now.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-start-block-09":{"type":"approved","suggestion":"","comment":"no changes"},"week-3-lesson-w3-start-block-06":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Noticing a part often opens a little space. The deeper shift is relational: what happens when you greet it with curiosity and understanding rather than criticism or impatience?","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-start-block-08":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"If you feel curious and accepting toward the part, you have access to Self. If you feel annoyed, afraid, or an urge to change the part, another part is present. That is not a problem. It is the next member of the system that needs to be welcomed and understood.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-start-block-11":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Guthrie uses loving gaze to describe a quality of attention that does not reduce a part to its role. It sees the effort, the fear, the loyalty, the intelligence and the cost. Love here does not mean pity or sentimentality. It means unconditional positive regard, accepting the part exactly as it is with no judgement of the part and no agenda for change. Relationship is available without conditions, on the part's terms.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-self-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"According to IFS, everyone has Self and that Self is fully developed, cannot be hurt, and is more available than commonly thought.  Access is often obscured because parts have blended with Self, which is the seat of awareness. You cannot create or improve Self; you can only uncover it.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-self-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Parts show up as thoughts, emotions, and body sensations. They provide the contents of consciousness. Self is the consciousness that notices the presence of parts and holds them in a relational field, characterised the eight C's.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-self-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Self-leadership does not mean becoming parts-free. It means enough access to Self for parts to contribute rather than carry the whole system alone.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-self-block-09":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Self, with a capital S, is our true identity. It is not our ego, our personality, or a super part. It is a capacity that transcends, embraces, and permeates all parts. One way to know Self is by the presence of the eight qualities that begin with the letter C, but Self is still more than that. Self is whole, wise, loving and good.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-self-block-10":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"We are born with Self, and it is already fully developed, but it can rarely be fully accessed until our bodies and brains reach full maturity.","comment":"Replace old text with next text."},"week-3-lesson-w3-self-block-11":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"delete this comment. material is covered above."},"week-3-lesson-w3-self-block-12":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Guthrie uses the term \"awake awareness\" to describe Self because Self is always aware and because noticing awareness -- that which experiences the world -- can be an easy way to access Self. The field practice from Week 1 prepares the ground on which parts can be met.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-self-block-15":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Knowing that Self is fully developed and undamaged can be empowering because it locates dignity and capacity in you rather than an expert.","comment":"replace with new text"},"week-3-lesson-w3-self-block-16":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Access to Self occurs on a continuum","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-self-block-06":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Clouds may obscure the sun but they cannot damage it. We do not have to attack the clouds in order to remember the ever-present light.\n\nSelf is the conductor, and parts are the players. Without the players, there is no sound; without the conductor, there is no harmony.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-self-block-14":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"We are born with Self, and Self is indestructible. Self is not created by achievement and not damaged by history. Access can be constrained or obscured when parts are carrying intense responsibilities or pain, but Self cannot be hurt, damaged or broken. Self is like the sky; the distress and trauma of our life are like the weather. The weather, no matter how violent, cannot hurt the sky. Likewise, the trauma we suffer cannot hurt Self.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-self-block-17":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"People sometimes turn Self into a perfect state that has to be maintained and that just increases pressure on your parts to perform. We teach that access to Self occurs on a continuum, and that access is always changing. A little curiosity about a fear or an anger opens a little access; a moment of clarity reminds us of what is possible; noticing when we are blended with parts helps us begin to shift. Progress comes from many small shifts over time. Let it be easy and fun. Effort indicates the presence of part -- a part that needs to be greeted with kindness.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-self-block-18":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Self-leadership does not mean that the parts disappear. Remember the metaphor of the of the orchestra: the parts are the players and Self is the conductor. Without the players, there is no sound; without the conductor there is no harmony. Leadership means listening to understand and integrating what you learn.","comment":""},"week-3-lesson-w3-self-block-20":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Guthrie places Self in conversation with contemplative traditions that speak of Buddha nature, Atman, divine presence and other forms of awakened awareness. The parallels can be meaningful. Participants may use secular language, spiritual language, or leave the question open.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-after-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"5.  Ask one question: \"How do you help?”","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-after-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Hope: “What if things could be different? What if you could work less, rest more, and do what you really like?” We are selling hope, but we are not demanding the protector buy it. They remain at choice.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-after-block-06":{"type":"approved","suggestion":"","comment":"quotation is good"},"week-4-lesson-w4-after-block-05":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Control: “You are the gatekeeper. We will not go anywhere without your opening the gate.” Control means genuine choice about pacing and process, including stopping the process. Guthrie states the principle plainly: “We will do nothing without your permission.”","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-boundary-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Do not push past protection   If pressure, blankness, panic, disorientation or concerns increase, stop. Return to ground, core and field. Respecting a “not now” is part of Self-leadership, not a failure of it.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-boundary-block-09":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Do not use gratitude to manipulate.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-boundary-block-03":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"There are no bad parts. A protector’s strategy can be costly while its intention is always positive.","comment":"change Keep with you to Remember"},"week-4-lesson-w4-boundary-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Respecting a positive intention is not permission to cause harm","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-boundary-block-05":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"You can appreciate a part’s protective intention and still interrupt the behaviour, remove access to a means of harm, leave the situation, make restitution or seek professional support. Compassion and respect for all belong together.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-boundary-block-06":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Taking care of ourselves and understanding protectors is no excuse for causing harm to another or allowing someone else to harm us. Being curious about parts is compatible with setting boundaries. Indeed, safety is paramount for all concerned.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-boundary-block-10":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"If you thank a protector only so that it will step aside, the system will likely experience the attempt as manipulation. Stop; find ground, core, and field. Then ask yourself, do I have access to curiosity? If no, stop. If yes, proceed.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-boundary-block-11":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Remember\n\nWhen a protector says no, respect the no. Respect builds trust, and building trust is more important than completing an exercise.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-live-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"IFS gives protectors priority because they carry the information about risk and capacity. During the live call, notice whether the work respects what is actually available, rather than treating the protector as a door to something more important behind it. Guthrie often says, “Slow is fast.\"","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-live-block-08":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"How does Guthrie respond to protector concerns, doubts, and silence?","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-live-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Validation: You have a good reason for what you do. Control: You set the pace, and you can stop at any time. Hope: There may be a future in which you work less and do more of what you prefer.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-live-block-05":{"type":"approved","suggestion":"","comment":"Quotation is correct and may be used wherever useful. Perhaps, separate the quotation from the paragraph and give it its own space."},"week-4-lesson-w4-live-block-06":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Validation means the strategy makes sense in its context. Control means the part can influence the pace and stop the process. Hope means another future may be possible, not that change is promised today. All three offers must be sincere; without sincerity, they are manipulation.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-firefighters-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Firefighters react to pain in the moment. They seek to douse the flames of distress with whatever is available right now.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-firefighters-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Leave\nDissociate, tune out, daydream, or physically depart.\n\nSoothe\nEat, take drugs, drink, watch television, look at pornography, shop, go to a spa, read, scroll, or turn to another activity for immediate relief.\n\nDischarge\nGet angry, argue, crack jokes, take impulsive action, or engage in high-energy and high-risk activities.\n\nShut down\nGo blank or numb, fall asleep, or faint.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-managers-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Because society often rewards these strategies, a person may experience the manager simply as “me”: the competent one, the responsible one, the one who never needs help. Beneath that is often the belief “I must hold everything together, and I must do it alone.”","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-managers-block-03":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Critic     Tries to improve performance before anyone else can criticize, humiliate or reject you.\n\nPerfectionist     Attempts to avoid shame by making sure every detail is correct.\n\nPleaser     Seeks to keep others happy so they don't lose the relationship.\n\nStriver      Aspires to achieve to prove their worth.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-managers-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Managers scan the future to prevent pain before it arises. They anticipate, criticise, perfect, please, control, achieve, caretake, and keep us functioning.","comment":"Change title to: Managers are committed to \"never again\"\n\nChange top paragraph to: Managers are dedicated to preventing bad things from happening again.  They tend to be controlling, critical, vigilant, and hardworking."},"week-4-lesson-w4-managers-block-05":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Organisation, standards, discernment, care, preparation and sustained effort, with the ability to step back and rest -- in short, optimal functioning.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-managers-block-10":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"A manager may be one of the most valued parts of a person at work or in a family. It can carry calendars, standards, money and responsibility. The difficulty is not the competence; it is the belief that the part is never allowed to stop. Guthrie describes managers as overburdened with responsibility beyond their capacity. They are trying to be Self, and only Self can be Self.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-managers-block-11":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"An inner critic notices errors quickly, and discernment may be its real gift. In an extreme role it attacks first, hoping that private humiliation will prevent the public kind. Simply demanding positive self-talk can leave it more alarmed. Healthy relationship with a critic begins by understanding it on its own terms, by realizing the depth of its fears and its commitment to the person.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-managers-block-13":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"How are you trying to help?\nDo you like your job? \nAre you tired?\nWhat are you afraid would happen if you stopped doing your job?\nWhat do you need?\nWhat is your deepest hope?\nWhat would you prefer to do?","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-managers-block-14":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"These are questions to ask inwardly, of your own part, and only when genuine curiosity is available. A question used to prove the manager unnecessary or needs to change will be heard as opposition and resisted. Remember all parts are good; all have positive intentions; they are simply waiting to be understood and respected. Then they can soften.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-cycle-block-09":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Use arrows and verbs. Keep the example current and low-intensity. Include the external context. Before starting, smile and invite an attitude of curiosity.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-cycle-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"1.  A manager criticises in an attempt to improve performance.\n\n2.  A vulnerable part hears the criticism and feels shame, fear, unworthiness, or hopelessness.\n\n3. A firefighter distracts from the distress by numbing, leaving or erupting.\n\n4.  The manager sees the firefighter’s response as dangerous and sharpens its criticism..\n\n5. The vulnerable one takes the criticism to heart and feels terrible, which re-activates the firefighter, which, in turn, triggers the manager, and so in an escalating vicious cycle.\n\n6.   Neither protector trusts that Self can lead.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-cycle-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Map an ordinary loop: overcommitting → resentment → distraction→ self-criticism. Ask what each protector is trying to do. Noticing the sequence is the practice, not stopping the loop.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-cycle-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"A protection cycle often consists of an external event, a vulnerable part feeling bad, a proactive attempt to manage the situation, reactive relief and internal backlash. Writing the order down can make the choice points easier to see.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-cycle-block-05":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"2.  A vulnerable part feels fear and shame.\n\n3. A manager looks for flaws in the feedback.\n\n4.  Shame breaks through anyway, and a firefighter numbs it with distraction, like binge-watching television.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-before-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"IFS focuses on three groups: managers, firefighters and exiles. Managers and firefighters are the protectors; they have jobs. Exiles are young, vulnerable parts who carry burdens: painful emotions like fear, sadness, and shame and limiting beliefs like unlovability, worthlessness, and being too much or too little. They do not have jobs; they are the ones the protectors seek to protect. That is the three-group ecology.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-before-block-05":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"This three-group ecology is a map, not a biological classification. It is a simplified and practical way to understand the dynamics of both internal and external human systems. Hold the map lightly, always engaging with curiosity and compassion.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-before-block-10":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The three-group ecology helps us understand how our inner system works. Managers anticipate problems and act to prevent them. Firefighters swing into action the moment a problem arises. Exiles are the ones that both managers and firefighters are trying to keep safe and out of harm's way. Apply these labels to your parts with a light touch, especially if you have not yet talked to the part. Approach all parts with curiosity and respect. Do not label other people's parts.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-before-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Firefighters     React when pain is triggered, using distraction, numbing, anger, dissociation, or other forms of escape, like drugs or bingeing on an activity.\n\nExiles     Young parts who carry wounds of hurt, fear, shame, unworthiness, loneliness, fear of being too much or too little.","comment":"Change the top paragraph to: Protective behavior can be dysfunctional. Our aim is not to criticize but to understand its original positive intention. By accepting the protector just as it is, it begins to change."},"week-4-lesson-w4-before-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Managers and firefighters want the same thing: avoid pain. But they take different approaches, work on different timelines, and dislike each other’s methods.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-before-block-08":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Regardless of how problematic their behaviour can be, managers and firefighters both have the same positive intentions: keep the internal world safe and pain-free. Neither is intrinsically better or worse than the other, though society often rewards managers and punishes firefighters. When they have been healed, they both offer gifts. Managers help us organize our lives, complete tasks, and achieve goals. Firefighters bring spontaneity, rest, and the ability to change gears.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-start-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Watch how Guthrie engages with protectors: with curiosity, understanding, and deep respect. Notice that he does not manipulate or attempt to change them.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-start-block-03":{"type":"approved","suggestion":"","comment":"Quotation is good as is and can be used wherever seems useful."},"week-4-lesson-w4-start-block-08":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Week 4 introduces two classes of protectors: managers and firefighters. Managers think ahead and act to prevent pain; they are proactive. Firefighters jump in to soothe or distract from pain that is already here; they are reactive. Two different strategies, same goal: avoid pain.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-start-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"By the end of this week    You will know about  two different approaches to protection: one strategic, looking ahead, considering the big picture; the other tactical, reacting in the moment, not concerned about consequences. You will also know to approach either class of protector with respect, humility, and a willingness to learn.","comment":"Title changes: Making Friends with Protectors"},"week-4-lesson-w4-start-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Protectors are not obstacles standing between you and the “real work”. Unblending and befriending protectors is the work. Building trust with protectors increases access to Self.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-start-block-06":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Protectors organise around pain: preventing it, containing it or ending it quickly. Their approaches can conflict, but they share the same orientation: avoiding pain.","comment":""},"week-4-lesson-w4-start-block-09":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Managers attempt to control the internal and external worlds so that we don't feel distressing emotions like fear, grief, anger, or shame. They anticipate problems and act to stop them before they develop. But despite their best efforts, the world has a way of triggering difficult emotions anyway. When that happens, firefighters step in and attempt to douse the flames of pain through dissociation, distraction or soothing.  Managers and firefighters mistrust each other. They are like sailors in a small boat: one thinks it needs to lean to starboard to keep the boat from capsizing, and the other thinks it needs to lean to port. What the boat needs is a captain: Self.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-after-general":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"This repeats previous material, though its positioning is useful. Do we keep, delete, or move? We don't want to unnecessarity alarm people. IFS is a natural and safe process; the human system is instinctually protective. While we want people to feel safe and behave responsibly, we don't want to scare them unnecessarily."},"week-5-lesson-w5-boundary-general":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"This is overkill, and it is going to annoy people with its overly cautious and highly repetitous approach. Delete this module or combine with previous module without making previous module longer."},"week-5-lesson-w5-capacity-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Sign of vulnerability     Sadness, fear, shame, loneliness, helplessness, or unexpected softness.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-capacity-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Skillful practice\n\nYou recognise enough to make a safe choice. Nothing has to be completed.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-capacity-block-09":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The question is not, “Can I tolerate more?” It is, “Do I have enough ground, support and choice to stay in relationship with what is here? Can I simply be with the feelings, letting them move, without trying to change them?\"","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-capacity-block-16":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Capacity question\n\nCan I stop, look around and choose a different activity right now? If the answer is no or unclear, stop and make contact with your environment.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-capacity-general":{"type":"approved","suggestion":"","comment":"The caution language has been reduced and simplified."},"week-5-lesson-w5-capacity-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The first skill is noticing what is happening, then deciding what comes next: whether to allow and accept or to step away.","comment":"Change title to: The first step is awareness"},"week-5-lesson-w5-capacity-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"You may feel something tender. A protector may or may not want distance. You can pause, feel your ground and simply be with the tenderness, or you can send love toward the vulnerability and move away.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-capacity-block-15":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"If intensity rises beyond your level of tolerance, tell the part you need to take a break. Restore contact with the present: open your eyes, name the date and the place, find edges and colours, press your feet into the floor, stand up, move, hum, and maybe contact another person.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-live-block-05":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"A respectful process, if it takes place at all, can show how vulnerability sits within the system, how protectors signal pace, and how Self-led presence differs from pushing for disclosure. It does not need to go deep to be valuable.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-live-block-02":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"Delete the Plan and Debrief sections. We are going overboard, creating more problems than we are solving.  We are not dealing with psychotics, just garden-variety neurotics. Also, there may not be time."},"week-5-lesson-w5-live-block-08":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"In the full IFS protocol, witnessing is what comes after a protector has given permission to talk to a vulnerable part, or exile. It involves hearing the exile's story and feeling some of its pain. Here, we are just offering an exile, if one appears, loving presence; we are not having an extended conversation. The process of fully witnessing and unburdening an exile is beyond the scope of this course.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-live-block-09":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"If you notice tenderness or vulnerability and a protector wants to stop, thank the protector for being direct and stop. Then feel the ground and orient in the room. Nothing else to do. The system’s limit was recognised and respected, which is a successful demonstration of Self-leadership.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-before-block-12":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"Delete this section. It opens up too much and settles little."},"week-5-lesson-w5-before-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Young, vulnerable parts carry hurt, pain, shame, fear, loneliness and unmet needs from our early years. Protectors work hard to keep that pain out of awareness.\n\nIFS uses the term \"exile\" for these young hurting parts because protectors have banished, or exiled, them to the unconscious. The protectors believe that feeling their distress would be too painful, destabilising or risky. In this introductory course, we usually say \"vulnerable part.\"","comment":"New title: You do not have to go looking for vulnerability"},"week-5-lesson-w5-before-block-03":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Beneath their burdens of pain, vulnerable parts hold many gifts: innocence, wonder, magic, sensitivity, play, joy, tenderness and the capacity for intimacy. This course does not treat vulnerability as a problem to be exposed or solved.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-before-block-05":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Managers try to keep vulnerable feeling from being activated. Firefighters act once it begins to break through. If vulnerability is near, the first move is to send compassion to the vulnerability, then to check in with the protectors who have concerns. If there is time, get to know those protectors.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-before-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"IFS defines exiles as parts sequestered within the system, either to protect them from further hurt or to protect the system from their pain. They are young and vulnerable, carrying emotions like fear, shame, and sadness and limiting beliefs like unworthiness or unlovability.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-before-block-08":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The term \"exile\" describes the part’s position in the system, and the ultimate intention is to bring them out of exile, though we will not teach you how to do that in this course. Guthrie emphasises that vulnerable parts have many gifts that enhance the delight of being alive. Think of healthy children at play.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-before-block-10":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"When we were young, we learned that certain feelings were not acceptable, that they put approval and safety at risk. So, managers banished those parts to the unconscious, and firefighters act when they escape into consciousness. The intensity of the protectors reflects the scale of the perceived risk. Put another way, the deeper the hurt, the more extreme the protectors.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-before-block-11":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"A friend cancels plans. A vulnerable part feels rejected and says, “I do not matter.” A manager explains that needing people is weak. A firefighter looks for a distraction. Meanwhile, the vulnerable one feels even more rejected.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-before-block-14":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"This section also seems unnecessary given all the explanation above. I suggest deleting it."},"week-5-lesson-w5-start-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"You can recognise signs of vulnerability, be with the parts that protect that vulnerability, help your system settle and orient to the present.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-start-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"This week explains more than it asks you to practise. Knowing the map should make it easier to access compassion and respect your protectors' concerns.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-start-block-01":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"The production note has been incorporated into the Week 5 title and introduction."},"week-5-lesson-w5-start-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Protectors organise around containing emotions they believe will be overwhelming, dangerous or unacceptable. Understanding that pattern can reduce shame: strong control or avoidance are protective strategies related to something that happened in the past.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-start-block-08":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"The model explains a great deal, and that can invite a search for a hidden wound behind every behaviour. That is not the task here. A present difficulty can often be addressed without delving deeply into the past. We can spend more time befriending protectors and building trust within the system.","comment":""},"week-5-lesson-w5-support-block-07":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"Delete this section."},"week-5-lesson-w5-support-block-11":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"delete this section"},"week-5-lesson-w5-support-general":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"The separate support-map module has been removed from the revised version; one short support boundary remains later in the week."},"week-6-lesson-w6-glossary-block-09":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Ground\nConnection to the earth through feet, seat, and physical support; surrendering to gravity; the first of Guthrie's building blocks of Presence.\n\nManager\nA protector that plans ahead to prevent pain or the activation of a vulnerable part; relies on control, inhibition, achievement, pleasing, criticism, task completion, and other pre-emptive strategies.\n\nPart\nA subpersonality, or submind, with its own perspective, emotions, sensations, impulses, hopes, fears, strategies and gifts.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-glossary-block-02":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"delete this paragrpah"},"week-6-lesson-w6-glossary-block-05":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Access to Self\nThe amount of Self that is available at a given moment. Access to Self is on a continuum: the useful questions are: How much access is available right now? Could I increase access just a little with grounding, conscious breathing, or smiling?\n\nBefriending\nBuilding a relationship with a part through curiosity, understanding, appreciation and respect for its concerns.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-glossary-block-11":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Self\nThe seat of awareness or essence of a person, associated with innate leadership qualities, including the eight Cs. Self is undamaged and fully developed in everyone. Guthrie also describes Self as awake awareness or Presence. The course keeps the English word throughout.\n\nSelf-led\nGuided by enough access to Self for parts to be heard, for circumstances to be considered, and for healthy choices to be made.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-glossary-block-13":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Three messages for protectors\nThey are validation, control and hope: validate the protector's positive intention, acknowledge that it controls pace and access to the internal system, and offer the hope of a healthier, freer, and more fulfilling future.\n\nVulnerable part\nThe synonym for exile, used in this course for a young part carrying hurt, shame, fear, loneliness or other unmet needs.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-language-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Try\n“A part of me wants to demand your attention.”\n\nThen add\n“What I want to ask for is 10 uninterrupted minutes of your time, either now or later today.”","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-language-block-13":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"A request leaves the other person room to say no. A demand takes choice away. A boundary states what you will do to protect yourself or your values; it does not diagnose or manage the other person’s inner system.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-live-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Arrive · 10 minutes\nReturn to ground, core and field, then name one thing that has become more usable during the course.\n\nReview · 15 minutes\nSummarise the core distinctions already taught: attention and awareness, Self and parts, protectors, and speaking on behalf of a part. Introduce no new model.\n\nDemonstrate and discuss · 35 minutes\nUse one ordinary relational example to show a pause, parts language, a clear request or boundary, and repair. Keep the focus on recognition rather than depth.\n\nIntegrate · 20 minutes\nParticipants identify one practice to continue, one relationship in which to experiment, and one form of support they may use.\n\nClose · 10 minutes\nQuestions, appreciation, and a final return to the shared field.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-boundary-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Explore further","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-boundary-block-06":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Six weeks can give you a coherent introduction, a set of experiential reference points, a shared vocabulary and several safe practices. They cannot resolve all inner conflict, complete trauma work, or confer competence to facilitate IFS.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-boundary-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Remember\nSelf-led does not mean parts-free. It means enough access to Self that parts can be heard, valued and invited to collaborate. It means having enough internal space to pause, then respond with calm, clarity, and compassion.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-boundary-block-10":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Self-led does not mean a life without parts. It means parts have increasing access to a relational field where they have access to calm, clarity, confidence, connection, curiosity, compassion, creativity, and  courage.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-relational-block-12":{"type":"approved","suggestion":"","comment":"quotation good as is. can be used wherever useful."},"week-6-lesson-w6-relational-block-14":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Being Self-led does not mean agreement.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-relational-block-05":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Access to Self can support a clear yes, a clear no, direct feedback and a firm boundary. More Self means more capacity. With more capacity, threat shrinks, judgements are less sticky, and what we are and are not responsible for is clear.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-relational-block-15":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Clarity and courage can support a direct no. Compassion may recognise another person’s pain without accepting their demand. Connectedness does not require continued contact. The marker is not niceness; it is being at choice.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-before-block-03":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"I notice without blending\n\nParts appear in my field of awareness without taking over.","comment":"Change \"Relationship is more familiar\" to \"I notice without blending\""},"week-6-lesson-w6-before-block-06":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Not yet noticing\nThe reaction feels like the whole of you and becomes visible only through its consequences.\n\nNoticing afterwards\nYou recognise the part and the sequence in reflection, even though there was no choice available at the time.\n\nNoticing during\nAwareness returns while the activation is still happening.\n\nI notice without blending\nParts appear in my field of awareness without taking over. I can hold them and speak for them without blending with them.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-before-block-09":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"What did you stop doing because it was not helping?","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-before-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Movement between these is not linear. Stress, poor sleep and circumstances can trigger a familiar pattern, and that does not erase what you have learned. The evidence worth tracking is whether recognition and repair become more available over time. Also, recovery occurs faster.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-library-general":{"type":"approved","suggestion":"","comment":"Looks good. No comments. 16 oct 2026"},"week-6-lesson-w6-start-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Live session: Periodically check to see if you have ground, core, and field. Notice how parts language can be used skilfully and, maybe, unskilfully. See how the IFS framework can support relationships.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-start-block-03":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"\"Our goal is to bring more Self to the world.\"","comment":"See re-write. Quotation may be used wherever useful."},"week-6-lesson-w6-start-block-04":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Integration means knowing which moves to make during your everyday life. One of the simplest is accessing curiosity -- about your experience, about another person's experience.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-start-block-06":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"As you go about your life, practice stopping and remembering the IFS concepts of Self and parts: before sending an email or text, during a disagreement, while making a decision, or after a reaction you would rather not repeat.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-start-block-01":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"By the end of this week\n\nYou will have a continuation plan you can realistically keep, one relationship in which you have tried speaking on behalf of a part, and a clearer sense of next step: practising alone, working with an IFS practitioner, joining a community, or continued study.","comment":"Change title to: A Self-led life\n\nChange text below title to: Week 6 brings together key principles and how that can be applied in all relationships: at home, at work, while socializing, in conflict, and making decisions."},"week-6-lesson-w6-start-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Integration can be easy and stress-free. It means noticing a part before acting from it, opening to the field when attention narrows, appreciating a protector without leaving it in control, and asking for support before capacity runs out.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-start-block-09":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"1. Attention selects, and awareness includes.\n2. A reaction is a part of you, not all of you.\n3. Befriending a part matters more than classifying it correctly.\n4. A protector’s behaviour makes sense once you know its positive intention.\n5. Work with vulnerable parts requires consent and capacity.\n6. Self-leadership becomes visible in the quality of your relationships.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-after-block-07":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Practices survive when they can get smaller. Design three versions now, before you need them. Here is an example:","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-after-block-14":{"type":"cut","suggestion":"","comment":"The revised examples replace the temporary editorial-status note."},"week-6-lesson-w6-after-block-02":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Weekly\nOne written conversation with a part.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-after-block-05":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Here are some common places for effective practice: noticing a protector before a conflict escalates; using parts language while speaking with a teenager; naming a perfectionistic reaction in a business partnership instead of turning the other person into the problem. Think of other places where noticing your parts can help you pause before responding.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-after-block-08":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Full\nOne weekly 15-minute reflection, one written parts map, one relational experiment.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-after-block-10":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"Solo practice supports awareness and everyday reflection. Community supports continuity, shared language and being witnessed, without peer therapy. A trained IFS practitioner — a coach, therapist or another qualified professional — can support goals, symptoms, relational patterns, trauma or deeper parts work within their competence and scope. Choose by fit, training and the work you want to do rather than by professional title alone. Formal training is a separate route for people who intend to facilitate IFS professionally.","comment":""},"week-6-lesson-w6-after-block-12":{"type":"rewrite","suggestion":"1. Practise ground, core and field for 30 seconds to five minutes on any day.\n2. Practise being curious about a familiar part, without trying to transform it.","comment":""}};
